**Proposition 9.1 (the three distinct optimization quantifiers).**
The following conclusions have different quantifiers and remain distinct.
For each public positive test input, (CA.32) has a common instrument
attainer. The known instrument (CA.43) or (CA.44) simultaneously attains
all positive diagonal minima, all three original means and the
worst-history optimum. Theorem 8.1 determines every resource vector
attaining any one of those means. None of these assertions evaluates
$\mathsf E_s(\rho)$ for every coherent $\rho$, or classifies the rest of
$\mathscr W_s$'s undominated boundary.

Proof. The first assertion is Corollary 6.4, whose design may depend on
the public test matrix but whose executed maps must work on every
unknown input. Proposition 7.3 gives the second assertion with one common
design; it concerns diagonal costs and original averages rather than all
individual coherent-state minima. Theorem 8.1 gives the third assertion
without restricting the original class. The matrix optimization (CA.28)
retains complex off-diagonal costs, two cone memberships and their plane
slacks. No preceding proof removes those variables or solves that
optimization on all coherent inputs. Likewise the sharp-average
condition cuts out only a specified part of the full resource frontier.

There is a concrete incompatibility with a simultaneous individual
minimizer at every exceptional strength. If a design minimized all twelve
original costs simultaneously, its vector would weakly dominate every
vector in the exceptional segment. Each segment point is undominated by
Corollary 8.4, so this vector would have to equal every segment point.
The segment has positive length and distinct vectors, a contradiction.
This is an obstruction to a least twelve-vector, not to the existence
of its separate coordinate minima. RE and IR's other pointwise/common
and nonleast results keep their respective stated hypotheses and ranges;
no larger range is inferred from this exceptional-strength argument.
$\square$

Within an exceptional equality segment the original worst-history cost
is strictly larger than $g_{27}(s)$ when $\theta\ne0$: one of the two
parities at height $27$ has cost
$g_{27}(s)+4|\theta|t\kappa\sqrt{27(64-27)}/128$.
Thus sharp means alone do not enforce sharp worst-history performance.
The $\theta=0$ common attainer retains current JR's sharp worst-history
law. This observation concerns the new equality vectors, not a new
worst-history optimum.

